---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Common Raven Identification — Field Guide';
const description = 'How to identify the Common Raven (Corvus corax): size, tail shape, bill, throat hackles, voice, and flight. All field marks with sourced measurements.';
const path        = '/species/common-raven/identification/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',          url: '/' },
  { name: 'Species',       url: '/species/' },
  { name: 'Common Raven',  url: '/species/common-raven/' },
  { name: 'Identification', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How do I identify a Common Raven?', a: 'The key field marks are: (1) wedge-shaped tail — the single most reliable mark; (2) large size — roughly Buzzard-sized, much larger than a Carrion Crow; (3) heavy arched bill; (4) shaggy throat hackles visible at close range; (5) deep croak "kronk" call; (6) soaring flight with aerial play. If you see a large black corvid soaring and tumbling over upland or coastal habitat in the UK, it is almost certainly a raven.' },
  { q: 'What is the best way to tell a raven from a crow?', a: 'Check the tail first: a wedge-shaped or diamond tail in flight identifies a raven; a fan-shaped tail is a crow. Voice is excellent at distance: a raven\'s call is a deep hollow "kronk"; a crow\'s call is a harsh "kaah." Size reinforces the identification: ravens are roughly twice the weight of Carrion Crows.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="id-page">
  <nav class="id-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Species · Field Identification</p>
    <h1>Common Raven — Identification</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle"><em>Corvus corax</em></p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Key identification features for the Common Raven: <strong>wedge-shaped tail</strong> (the most reliable single mark), large size (56–69 cm), heavy arched bill, shaggy throat hackles, deep croak call, and soaring flight with aerial play. In the UK, any large black corvid soaring in upland, moorland, or coastal habitat is very likely a raven.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Measurements</h2>
    <div class="id-table-wrap">
      <table class="id-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Length</td><td>56–69 cm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wingspan</td><td>120–150 cm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Weight</td><td>689–1,625 g</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bill length</td><td>5.5–7.5 cm (culmen)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="id-src-note">Source: Madge &amp; Burn (1994).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Key Field Marks</h2>

    <h3>1. Tail Shape — the Most Reliable Mark</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven's tail is <strong>wedge-shaped or diamond-shaped</strong> in flight — the central tail feathers are longer than the outer ones, creating a pointed or wedge profile. This contrasts directly with the fan-shaped or rounded tail of Carrion Crow, Rook, and Jackdaw. The wedge tail is visible at considerable distance and is the fastest single identification mark.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>

    <h3>2. Size</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> At 56–69 cm and 689–1,625 g, the Common Raven is roughly twice the weight of a Carrion Crow (370–650 g) and comparable in size to a Common Buzzard. When directly compared with a crow, the size difference is obvious. At distance without a size reference, the proportions — heavy-bodied, large-headed, long-tailed — still read "big" compared to other corvids.</p>

    <h3>3. Bill</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The raven's bill is <strong>heavy, arched, and deep at the base</strong> — substantially larger than a crow's bill and with a pronounced curve from culmen to tip. The nasal bristles (feathers at the base of the bill) are long and project forward, adding to the distinctive facial profile visible at close range.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>

    <h3>4. Throat Hackles</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> At close range, the raven's throat shows <strong>shaggy, lanceolate (pointed) hackle feathers</strong> that hang loosely and can be erected. These are unique to ravens among British corvids — Carrion Crow, Rook, Jackdaw, and Hooded Crow all have smooth throat feathers. In aerial display and vocalisation, the hackles are particularly visible.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>

    <h3>5. Plumage</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Entirely black, with a blue-green iridescent gloss visible in good light. Fresh plumage (autumn) shows stronger iridescence; worn summer plumage appears browner-black. No white, grey, or pale markings at any age.</p>

    <h3>6. Voice</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The standard call is a deep, hollow <strong>"kronk"</strong> or <strong>"pruuk"</strong> — a resonant croak clearly distinct from the caws and kaah calls of other corvids. The call carries well and can identify a raven at considerable distance before the bird is seen. The vocal repertoire is much wider (see: <a href="/behaviour/raven-vocalisation/">Raven Vocalisation</a>) but the basic croak is diagnostic.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>

    <h3>7. Flight</h3>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens <strong>soar extensively</strong> on flat, slightly bowed wings — unlike the more direct flapping flight of Carrion Crows. In good conditions they ride thermals and updrafts for extended periods. They frequently engage in aerial play — rolling, tumbling, chasing in pairs or groups. This playing, soaring flight over upland habitat is a highly reliable field indicator. Crows do not play aerially in this way.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Confusion Species</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs Crow — full guide</a> (tail, size, bill, voice, hackles, flight, habitat)</li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-carrion-crow/">Raven vs Carrion Crow</a> (UK-specific)</li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-rook/">Raven vs Rook</a> (pale face, colonial nesting)</li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-jackdaw/">Raven vs Jackdaw</a> (much smaller; pale eye; grey nape)</li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-hooded-crow/">Raven vs Hooded Crow</a> (grey body — not an all-black bird)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="id-sources id-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="id-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Madge, S. &amp; Burn, H. (1994). <em>Crows and Jays</em>. Christopher Helm. — all measurements and field marks.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Boarman, W.I. &amp; Heinrich, B. (1999). Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). In Poole, A. &amp; Gill, F. (eds.), <em>The Birds of North America</em>, No. 476. — flight behaviour and vocalisation.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="id-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> field-guide / species account data</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="id-nav-bottom">
    <a href="/species/common-raven/" class="id-back">← Common Raven Overview</a>
    <a href="/species/common-raven/range-and-habitat/" class="id-next">Range and Habitat →</a>
  </nav>
</article>
</BaseLayout>

